A||||8||Formula||Clear Tail light Instructions||||1||
Z||000000||Formula||03-06-2001||11:10 PM||||I made this elaboate so that people could use it on their websites.  If you want to use it on a website then feel free to.<BR>_________________________________________<P>1. Find a pattern that you want for your tail lights, circles, bats, little fieros, etc. You need to cover the majority of the actual tailight with vinyl or else it will look like the tail lights are broken out. <BR>2. Remove the tail light buckets (2). There should be three long screws in the top that you remove with a phillips screw driver. <BR>3. Carefully disassemble the tail light buckets with a medium sized flat head screw driver and a lot of patience. The buckets have snap clips holding them together that break easily, there is also a lot of weather stripping inside so BE CAREFUL. <BR>**If you do happen to break a clip then glue it back together with a very strong glue and put some sealant around it** <BR>4. Remove the Red and White inserts. While your at it go ahead and clean the inside of the tail light lense real good, its going to be A LOT more visible. Do not use anything with ammonia, this could cause the vinyl to not stick properly and make the whole project a failure. <BR>5. Go to an Automotive Vinyl shop, or a Vinyl Sign maker and get them to put the vinyl on in one of three ways. <BR>A. Your chosen pattern on the outside. Make it go up over the black plastic some for a flush factory look. Light shines through dimly. <BR>B. On the inside only. This lets light shine through some more, especially on the outer edges. This would be the worst one to do. <BR>C. Both A and B together, no light shines through. This is by far the best way to go. <BR>6. Go to an Automotive Off-Road shop, A Catalog such as J.C. Whitney, or a Customizing shop and get Red tail lights. The outside ones have to be red to make it not so noticeable to law enforcement, the 2 in between the red and white can be Amber or Red, and the inside 2 need to be Reverse lights in White or Super White. <BR>7. Buy weather stripping, sealant, etc. whatever works for you best and put it in the crackes where the two assemblies go together. <BR>8. Clip all the clips and onc together further repair any damaged ones. <BR>9. Replace the tail light bulbs with the red ones. <BR>10. Put the tailights back in and screw down.<BR>Price Estimates: <BR>1 phillips screwdriver $1.00 <BR>1 flathead screwdriver $1.00 <BR>6 new tailights $6.00 <BR>Inside Vinyl $10.00 <BR>Outside Vinyl $10.00 <BR>Sealant $2.00 <BR>Glue (if needed) $1.00 <BR>Max Price: $40.00 (varies with local prices) <BR>Min Price: $20.00<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/yellowtored3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
